## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of graph reachability and fixed-point semantics in logical implications, with practical applications in policy and permission systems.

## Key Concepts
- Upward-closure in partially ordered sets
- Fixed-point computation via forward-closure
- Linear-time graph traversal algorithms
- Cycle handling through visited state management

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(T, edges) {
  const trueSet = new Set(T);
  const queue = [...T];
  const adjacency = new Map();

  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    if (!adjacency.has(a)) adjacency.set(a, []);
    adjacency.get(a).push(b);
  }

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const x = queue.shift();
    for (const y of adjacency.get(x) || []) {
      if (!trueSet.has(y)) {
        trueSet.add(y);
        queue.push(y);
      }
    }
  }

  return trueSet;
}
```

## Example
Consider flags {A,B,C,D,E,F} with relations: A→B, B→C, C→A (cycle), A→D, D→E, E→F, B→F. If T={A}, propagation yields {A,B,C,D,E,F} as the least model containing T.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about combined implications and negation, and to design a small exhaustive search with pruning.

## Key Concepts

- Implication and negation in boolean constraints
- Exhaustive search with pruning
- Small-scope backtracking for correctness checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve() {
  for (let A = 0; A <= 1; A++) {
    for (let B = 0; B <= 1; B++) {
      for (let C = 0; C <= 1; C++) {
        const ok1 = (!A) || B;     // A -> B
        const ok2 = (!B) || (!C);  // B -> ¬C
        const ok3 = (A || B || C); // at least one true
        if (ok1 && ok2 && ok3) return {A,B,C};
      }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with Kleene three-valued logic in a small constraint system and to design backtracking propagation that respects non-classical truth tables.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with T>U>F
- Implication defined as ¬a ∨ b
- Propagation and backtracking in 3-valued CSPs

## Code Example

```javascript
function neg(x){ return x==="T"?"F": x==="F"?"T":"U"; }
function or(a,b){ return (a==="T"||b==="T")?"T": (a==="U"||b==="U")?"U":"F"; }
function and(a,b){ return (a==="F"||b=="F")?"F": (a==="U"||b==="U")?"U":"T"; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = neg(a);
  return or(na,b);
}
```

